Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 01:00:00 +0300
Subject: Audio: playback period of 1024 frames (SFML_AUDIO_PERIOD_FRAMES)

SFML 3.1 opens its miniaudio playback device with periodSizeInFrames = 64 and leaves the
period count at miniaudio's 3: a device buffer of 192 frames, 4 ms. On the PS3 (ALSA through
dmix, no realtime scheduling for the game's audio thread) the PPE misses a 4 ms deadline
whenever the game does anything heavy, and every miss is an xrun: Extreme Tux Racer logged 608
in a 2 minute played session (7.5 dropouts a minute).

The period is now 1024 frames, 3 periods, about 64 ms of buffer at 48 kHz (23 ms at the
period), 24 KB of f32 stereo. SFML_AUDIO_PERIOD_FRAMES (32 to 16384) overrides it; 64 gives
upstream's value back. The device buffer is the latency of every sound effect and of the music,
so the period stays well under a frame-rate-critical 100 ms.

diff --git a/src/SFML/Audio/AudioDevice.cpp b/src/SFML/Audio/AudioDevice.cpp
--- a/src/SFML/Audio/AudioDevice.cpp
+++ b/src/SFML/Audio/AudioDevice.cpp
@@ -36,6 +36,7 @@
 #include <unordered_map>
 
 #include <cassert>
+#include <cstdlib>
 
 
 namespace sf::priv
@@ -718,7 +719,15 @@
     // If this does cause issues we would have to expose setting this value
     // through the public API so the developer can set it to an appropriate
     // value depending on the audio data they intend to play
-    playbackDeviceConfig.periodSizeInFrames = 64;
+    playbackDeviceConfig.periodSizeInFrames = 1024;
+
+    // Walnut: SFML_AUDIO_PERIOD_FRAMES overrides the period (32 to 16384 frames; 64 is upstream's)
+    if (const char* env = std::getenv("SFML_AUDIO_PERIOD_FRAMES"))
+    {
+        const long frames = std::strtol(env, nullptr, 10);
+        if (frames >= 32 && frames <= 16384)
+            playbackDeviceConfig.periodSizeInFrames = static_cast<std::uint32_t>(frames);
+    }
 
     if (const auto result = ma_device_init(&*m_context, &playbackDeviceConfig, &*m_playbackDevice); result != MA_SUCCESS)
     {
